<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oxford Movement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1830s</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Tractarianism, Tractarians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> Movement was a high-church movement within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>, begun in the 1830s by clergy mostly associated with the University of Oxford, which developed into Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. Prompted by the Irish Church Temporalities Bill and John Keble's Assize Sermon of 1833, its leaders, among them Keble, John Henry Newman and Edward Bouverie Pusey, published the Tracts for the Times, arguing that the church's authority came from God through apostolic succession rather than from the state. Newman's reception into the Catholic Church in 1845 shook the movement, yet it led to Anglican religious orders, a more central place for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a> and the wider use of vestments.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oxford-movement.jpg" alt="Oxford Movement" width="330" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rosa Corder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rosa_Corder_(1853-1893)_-_Reverend_Edward_Bouverie_Pusey,_Member_of_Keble_College_Council_(1870–1880)_(copy_of_an_earlier_work)_-_PCF31_-_Keble_College.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Hymnal Noted, made up entirely of Latin hymns in English with music drawn mainly from plainchant, was a product of the <b>Oxford Movement</b> in the Church of England, and the hymn was taken up for its medieval air and its fit to the season.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-come-o-come-emmanuel">O Come, O Come, Emmanuel</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1834 John Keble, one of the leaders of the <b>Oxford Movement</b> in the Church of England, translated it into English as &quot;Hail, Gladdening Light&quot;, keeping the irregular metre of the original.</blockquote><a href="/songs/phos-hilaron">Phos Hilaron (O Gladsome Light)</a></li></ul></section>
